<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>writing</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>writing</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Email Writing</li><li>Essay Writing</li><li>Letter Writing</li><li>Notice Writing</li><li>Paragraph Writing</li><li>Report Writing</li></ul><h2>Email Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Email Writing?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Email writing is the composition of electronic messages for personal or professional communication. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, email writing (often grouped with letter writing) carries 5-8 marks. While emails share similarities with formal and informal letters, they have distinctive features: a subject line , shorter body, and a more modern structure. Understanding the conventions of email writing is essential for both academic and real-world communication.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Structure of a Formal Email</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A formal email follows this structure: (1) Subject line — a concise phrase stating the purpose (e.g., &#x27;Subject: Application for Scholarship Programme&#x27;). (2) Salutation : &#x27;Dear Sir/Madam&#x27; or &#x27;Dear Mr. Sharma&#x27;. (3) Opening — state the purpose clearly in the first sentence. (4) Body — provide relevant details in 2-3 short paragraphs. (5) Closing — &#x27;Yours faithfully&#x27; (unknown name) or &#x27;Yours sincerely&#x27; (known name). (6) Signature — full name and contact information. The body should be 80-120 words for NEB exams. Unlike letters, emails do not require sender/receiver addresses at the top.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Structure of an Informal Email</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>An informal email to a friend or relative is less rigid: (1) Subject line — optional but recommended (e.g., &#x27;Subject: Weekend Plans&#x27;). (2) Salutation : &#x27;Dear Ram,&#x27; &#x27;Hi Sita,&#x27;. (3) Opening — personal greeting and well-wishes (&#x27;I hope you are doing well&#x27;). (4) Body — share news, ask questions, express feelings in a warm tone. (5) Closing : &#x27;Write soon&#x27;, &#x27;Best wishes&#x27;, &#x27;Your friend&#x27;. (6) Signature — first name only. Contractions, colloquial expressions, and exclamations are acceptable. The body should be 80-100 words for NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Email vs. Letter: Key Differences</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Key differences between email and letter writing: (1) Addresses — letters require sender&#x27;s and receiver&#x27;s addresses; emails do not. (2) Subject line — emails require a subject line; letters do not. (3) Length — emails are generally shorter and more concise. (4) Speed — emails are sent immediately; letters involve postal delay. (5) Formality — both formal and informal emails exist, but even formal emails tend to be slightly less rigid than formal letters. (6) Attachments — emails can include files; letters cannot. In NEB exams, if the question specifies &#x27;email&#x27;, follow email format; if it says &#x27;letter&#x27;, follow letter format. Using the wrong format costs marks.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Subject Line: The Most Important Element</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The subject line is unique to email and is critical for effective communication. A good subject line is: (1) Concise — 5-10 words maximum. (2) Informative — it should tell the reader exactly what the email is about. (3) Specific — avoid vague subjects like &#x27;Hello&#x27; or &#x27;Important&#x27;. Examples: &#x27;Subject: Request for Leave on 15th November&#x27; (good) vs. &#x27;Subject: Something important&#x27; (bad). In NEB exams, omitting the subject line in an email question is a common error that costs marks. The subject line should be underlined or placed on its own line.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Common Email Topics in NEB Exams</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Frequently assigned email topics include: (1) Formal email to principal — requesting permission, applying for something, complaining about facilities. (2) Formal email to editor — raising a civic issue. (3) Informal email to friend — inviting, congratulating, sharing news. (4) Informal email to relative — family news, festival greetings, apologies. (5) Professional email — job application, enquiry about a course, product complaint. For each type, the format and tone change appropriately. Students must identify the email type from the question prompt and apply the correct conventions.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Email vs. Letter format: Emails do not require sender&#x27;s or receiver&#x27;s addresses at the top. Emails require a subject line; letters do not. Emails use &#x27;Yours faithfully/sincerely&#x27; like formal letters, but informal emails use casual closings like &#x27;Love&#x27; or &#x27;Your friend&#x27;. The key difference is the subject line — it is mandatory for emails and absent in letters.</td></tr><tr><td>Subject line placement: In emails, the subject line appears at the top, after the salutation or before it. In NEB exams, it is typically placed after the salutation: &#x27;Dear Sir/Madam, Subject: ....&#x27; The subject line should be concise (5-10 words) and descriptive. Forgetting the subject line is the most common error in email writing questions.</td></tr><tr><td>Formal email vs. Formal letter: Formal emails are shorter and more direct than formal letters. They omit addresses but include a subject line. The tone is similarly formal in both, but emails are generally more concise. In NEB exams, if the question says &#x27;email&#x27;, do not write addresses; if it says &#x27;letter&#x27;, do include addresses.</td></tr><tr><td>Informal email vs. Informal letter: Informal emails are essentially the same as informal letters in tone and content. The main difference is the subject line (present in emails, absent in letters) and the absence of addresses (present in letters, absent in emails). Otherwise, both use warm, personal language and first-name signatures.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Essay Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is an Essay?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>An essay is a structured piece of writing that presents the author&#x27;s argument, analysis, or description on a particular topic. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, essay writing is one of the most important components, typically carrying 10-15 marks. A well-written essay has a clear introduction (opening paragraph that introduces the topic), a well-organised body (multiple paragraphs developing the main points), and a concise conclusion (closing paragraph that summarises the main ideas and leaves a final impression).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Types of Essays</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Essays are classified into four main types: Narrative essay — tells a story or recounts an event (e.g., &#x27;My most memorable experience&#x27;). Descriptive essay — paints a picture with words, describing a person, place, or thing (e.g., &#x27;My favourite season&#x27;, &#x27;A rainy day in Kathmandu&#x27;). Expository essay — explains or informs about a topic using facts and examples (e.g., &#x27;The importance of education&#x27;, &#x27;How to prevent pollution&#x27;). Argumentative/Persuasive essay — presents a viewpoint and supports it with reasoning and evidence (e.g., &#x27;Should mobile phones be banned in schools?&#x27;). NEB exams typically require expository or argumentative essays.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Structure of a Good Essay</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A good essay follows a three-part structure: (1) Introduction — begin with a hook (interesting opening sentence), provide background context, and state the thesis statement (main argument or purpose). The introduction should be 10-15% of the total length. (2) Body paragraphs — each paragraph should focus on one main idea, supported by examples, explanations, or evidence. Use topic sentences to introduce each paragraph&#x27;s main point. Aim for 3-5 body paragraphs. (3) Conclusion — restate the thesis in different words, summarise the main points, and end with a final thought, recommendation, or call to action. The conclusion should be 10% of the total length.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Planning and Outlining</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Before writing, spend 3-5 minutes planning your essay. Jot down the main points you want to make, arrange them in logical order, and decide on a thesis statement. A simple outline helps prevent rambling and ensures coherence. For argumentative essays, consider both sides of the issue before presenting your position. For expository essays, list the key points you will explain. Good planning saves time during the actual writing and produces a more organised, coherent essay.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Writing a Strong Introduction</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>The introduction is the first impression your essay makes. A strong introduction: (1) Grabs attention — start with a question, statistic, quote, or vivid statement. &#x27;Did you know that over 80% of Nepali students use smartphones daily?&#x27; (2) Provides context — give brief background information about the topic. (3) States the thesis — clearly express your main argument or purpose. Avoid starting with dictionary definitions (&#x27;According to the dictionary, pollution is...&#x27;) — this is weak and clichéd. Your introduction should be 3-5 sentences for a standard essay.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Writing Effective Body Paragraphs</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Each body paragraph should follow the PEEL structure: P oint (topic sentence stating the main idea), E vidence (examples, facts, or data supporting the point), E xplanation (explain how the evidence supports your point), L ink (connect back to the thesis or to the next paragraph). Transitions between paragraphs are essential for coherence: &#x27;Furthermore&#x27;, &#x27;In addition&#x27;, &#x27;On the other hand&#x27;, &#x27;However&#x27;, &#x27;Consequently&#x27;, &#x27;For example&#x27;. Each body paragraph should be 80-120 words for a standard essay. Avoid including too many ideas in one paragraph — one main idea per paragraph is the golden rule.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Letter Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Letter Writing?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Letter writing is the art of composing written communication to convey information, express feelings, or make requests. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, letter writing is a heavily tested skill, typically carrying 8-12 marks. Students are expected to write two main types of letters: Formal letters (to authorities, editors, principals) and Informal letters (to friends, relatives). Each type has a distinct format, tone, and structure that must be followed precisely.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Formal Letter: Format and Structure</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A formal letter follows a strict format: (1) Sender&#x27;s address (top left, no date below). (2) Date (below sender&#x27;s address). (3) Receiver&#x27;s designation and address (below date). (4) Salutation : &#x27;Sir/Madam&#x27; (if name is unknown) or &#x27;Dear Mr. Sharma&#x27; (if name is known). (5) Subject : A concise line stating the purpose, preceded by &#x27;Subject:&#x27; (e.g., &#x27;Subject: Complaint about irregular water supply&#x27;). (6) Body : Three paragraphs — opening (state purpose), middle (explain details), closing (request action/thank). (7) Complimentary close : &#x27;Yours faithfully&#x27; (if salutation is &#x27;Sir/Madam&#x27;) or &#x27;Yours sincerely&#x27; (if salutation uses a name). (8) Signature and name .</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Formal Letter: Tone and Language</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Formal letters require a respectful, objective, and concise tone. Use formal language: no contractions (&#x27;do not&#x27; not &#x27;don&#x27;t&#x27;), no slang, no emotional language. Be direct and clear — state the purpose in the first paragraph. Use polite expressions: &#x27;I would like to draw your attention to...&#x27;, &#x27;I kindly request you to...&#x27;, &#x27;I would be grateful if...&#x27;. Avoid imperative sentences (&#x27;Fix the problem now!&#x27;) — use polite requests instead (&#x27;I request you to look into this matter&#x27;). Sign off with &#x27;Yours faithfully&#x27; (unknown name) or &#x27;Yours sincerely&#x27; (known name). The body should be 100-150 words for NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Common Formal Letter Topics in NEB</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Frequently assigned formal letter topics include: (1) Letter to the editor — complaining about a civic issue (garbage disposal, water shortage, traffic, noise pollution). (2) Letter to the principal — requesting permission for an event, complaining about facilities, suggesting improvements. (3) Letter of complaint — to a company about defective products or poor service. (4) Letter of application — applying for a job or scholarship. (5) Letter of enquiry — asking for information about a course, product, or service. For each type, the format remains the same; only the content and specific expressions change.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Informal Letter: Format and Structure</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>An informal letter to a friend or relative is less rigid in format but still follows a conventional structure: (1) Sender&#x27;s address (top right or left). (2) Date . (3) S salutation : &#x27;Dear Ram,&#x27; &#x27;Dear Sita,&#x27; &#x27;Dear Mama,&#x27; &#x27;Dear Bhaija.&#x27; (4) Opening : Inquire about the recipient&#x27;s well-being (&#x27;I hope this letter finds you in good health&#x27;). (5) Body : Share news, express feelings, ask questions — written in a warm, personal tone. Use contractions (&#x27;I&#x27;m&#x27;, &#x27;don&#x27;t&#x27;), exclamations, and casual expressions. (6) Closing : &#x27;Write soon&#x27;, &#x27;Give my regards to...&#x27;, &#x27;Love&#x27;, &#x27;Your friend&#x27;. (7) Signature : First name only (e.g., &#x27;Sita&#x27;). The body should be 100-120 words for NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Informal Letter: Tone and Language</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Informal letters use a warm, personal, and conversational tone. You can use contractions, colloquial expressions, exclamations, and even abbreviations. Express genuine interest in the recipient&#x27;s life. Share personal news, ask about family, reminisce about shared experiences. Use phrases like: &#x27;How have you been?&#x27;, &#x27;I can&#x27;t believe...&#x27;, &#x27;Guess what happened!&#x27;, &#x27;I miss you&#x27;, &#x27;Write back soon&#x27;. The key difference from formal letters is the emotional warmth and personal touch. Avoid formal language, rigid structure, or overly polite expressions.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Notice Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is a Notice?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A notice is a brief, formal announcement posted in a public place or distributed to inform a specific group of people about an event, rule, or important information. In the NEB Class 11 English examination, notice writing is a commonly tested skill, typically carrying 5-8 marks. A well-written notice is concise, clear, and follows a standard format that includes a heading, date, body, and issuing authority.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Format of a Notice</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The standard notice format includes: (1) Heading/Title — the word &#x27;NOTICE&#x27; centred at the top in capital letters. (2) Issuer&#x27;s name/organisation — the name of the school, institution, or authority issuing the notice. (3) Date — the date of issue (usually placed below the heading or at the bottom left). (4) Subject/Heading line — a concise title describing the notice&#x27;s purpose (e.g., &#x27;Annual Sports Day&#x27;, &#x27;School Closure Notice&#x27;). (5) Body — the main content: what the notice is about, when and where it applies, any instructions or actions required. (6) Signature and designation — the name and position of the person issuing the notice (e.g., &#x27;Principal&#x27;, &#x27;School Captain&#x27;, &#x27;Head Boy&#x27;). The body should be 50-80 words — concise and to the point.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Key Features of a Notice</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A notice must be: (1) Concise — every word matters; avoid unnecessary details. (2) Clear — the message should be understood immediately. (3) Precise — include exact dates, times, and venues. (4) Formal — use formal language; no contractions, slang, or emotional language. (5) Action-oriented — state clearly what readers need to do (e.g., &#x27;All students are requested to...&#x27;, &#x27;Participation is mandatory for...&#x27;). A notice is not an essay — it should not include explanations, anecdotes, or lengthy descriptions.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Types of Notices</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Common types of notices in NEB exams include: (1) Event notice — announcing a school event (sports day, function, visit). &#x27;Annual Sports Day is scheduled for 15th November 2024. All students are required to participate.&#x27; (2) Closure notice — informing about school closure. &#x27;The school will remain closed on 15th August on account of Janai Purnima.&#x27; (3) Instruction notice — giving specific instructions. &#x27;All students must submit their project reports by Friday.&#x27; (4) Lost and found notice — reporting a lost item or found object. &#x27;A blue school bag has been found in the library. The owner may claim it from the office.&#x27; (5) Safety notice — warning about a hazard. &#x27;Caution: Wet floor. Please exercise care.&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Notice Writing Tips for NEB Exams</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>To score well in notice writing: (1) Follow the format precisely — missing any element (date, heading, signature) costs marks. (2) Keep the body within 50-80 words — too short loses content marks; too long wastes time. (3) Use formal, impersonal language — &#x27;It is informed that...&#x27; or &#x27;All students are requested to...&#x27; (4) Include all necessary details — date, time, venue, action required. (5) Write the issuing authority&#x27;s name and designation — e.g., &#x27;Ramesh Sharma, School Captain&#x27;. (6) Underline the heading — the word &#x27;NOTICE&#x27; should be underlined and centred. These conventions are strictly followed by NEB examiners.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Common Mistakes in Notice Writing</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Avoid these errors: (1) Forgetting the word &#x27;NOTICE&#x27; at the top. (2) Writing in the first person (&#x27;I have decided...&#x27;) — notices are impersonal. (3) Using contractions (&#x27;don&#x27;t&#x27;, &#x27;can&#x27;t&#x27;). (4) Making the body too long — a notice is not an essay. (5) Forgetting the date — essential for any formal notice. (6) Not mentioning the issuing authority — always sign off with name and designation. (7) Using emotional language — notices are factual and formal. (8) Writing an incomplete sentence — every sentence should be grammatically complete.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Notice vs. Letter: A notice is a public announcement meant for a broad audience (all students, all residents). A letter is a private communication to a specific person or authority. Notices are posted publicly; letters are sent privately. Notices use formal, impersonal language; letters (especially informal ones) use personal language.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice vs. Advertisement: A notice informs about an event, rule, or loss. An advertisement promotes a product, service, or event to attract customers. Notices are issued by institutions (schools, colleges, government offices). Advertisements are issued by businesses or individuals. Notices are formal; advertisements are persuasive and may use creative language.</td></tr><tr><td>Notice vs. Report: A notice announces information to a specific group. A report provides a detailed account of an event, investigation, or situation, usually for an authority. Notices are brief (50-80 words); reports are longer (150-200 words) and include observations, findings, and recommendations.</td></tr><tr><td>Date placement in notices: The date can appear either below the heading (top right or left) or at the bottom left of the notice. Both placements are acceptable in NEB exams, but consistency is key — do not place the date in different positions in different notices.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Paragraph Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is a Paragraph?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A paragraph is a group of related sentences that develop a single central idea. It is the basic unit of composition in English writing. A well-written paragraph has three essential elements: a topic sentence (states the main idea), supporting sentences (provide details, examples, explanations), and a concluding sentence (summarises or transitions). In NEB English exams, paragraph writing questions typically carry 5-8 marks and require students to write a coherent paragraph on a given topic within 100-150 words.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>The Topic Sentence</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The topic sentence is usually the first sentence of the paragraph and states the main idea that the paragraph will develop. It should be specific enough to guide the paragraph but broad enough to allow development. A good topic sentence: &#x27;Regular exercise has numerous physical and mental health benefits.&#x27; A weak topic sentence: &#x27;Exercise is good.&#x27; The topic sentence acts as a promise to the reader about what the paragraph will discuss. In NEB exams, a clear topic sentence is essential for earning marks for organisation and coherence.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Supporting Sentences</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Supporting sentences develop the main idea introduced in the topic sentence. They can provide: examples (e.g., &#x27;For instance, running improves cardiovascular health&#x27;), details (e.g., &#x27;A 30-minute walk daily can reduce the risk of heart disease by 30%&#x27;), explanations (e.g., &#x27;Exercise releases endorphins, which improve mood and reduce stress&#x27;), facts/statistics (e.g., &#x27;According to WHO, physical inactivity is the fourth leading risk factor for global mortality&#x27;). Each supporting sentence should relate directly to the topic sentence. Unrelated sentences weaken the paragraph&#x27;s coherence and cost marks.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>The Concluding Sentence</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The concluding sentence wraps up the paragraph by restating the main idea in different words, summarising the key points, or providing a final thought. It should not introduce new information. Examples: &#x27;Therefore, incorporating regular physical activity into daily routine is essential for maintaining overall health.&#x27; &#x27;In summary, the benefits of exercise extend beyond physical fitness to mental well-being as well.&#x27; The concluding sentence gives the paragraph a sense of closure and helps the reader remember the main point.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Coherence and Unity</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Coherence means the paragraph flows logically — ideas connect smoothly and readers can follow the progression. Unity means every sentence relates to the single main idea. Achieving coherence requires: (1) Logical order — arrange supporting sentences in a sensible sequence (chronological, spatial, order of importance, cause-effect). (2) Transitional words — use connectors like &#x27;first&#x27;, &#x27;second&#x27;, &#x27;moreover&#x27;, &#x27;however&#x27;, &#x27;for example&#x27;, &#x27;consequently&#x27;, &#x27;in addition&#x27;. (3) Pronoun reference — use pronouns (it, they, this, these) to avoid repetition and create links between sentences. A paragraph lacking coherence reads like a list of unrelated statements.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Types of Paragraphs</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Paragraphs can be classified by their purpose: Descriptive paragraph — paints a picture using sensory details (sight, sound, smell, touch, taste). &#x27;The marketplace was a vibrant tapestry of colours and sounds — piles of bright red tomatoes, the pungent aroma of cumin and turmeric, the chatter of vendors hawking their wares.&#x27; Narrative paragraph — tells a short story or recounts an event. &#x27;That morning, I woke up to find the streets covered in a thin layer of snow, a rare sight in Kathmandu.&#x27; Expository paragraph — explains or informs about a topic. &#x27;Renewable energy sources, such as solar and hydroelectric power, are increasingly vital to Nepal&#x27;s energy security.&#x27; Argumentative paragraph — presents a claim with supporting evidence. &#x27;School uniforms should be mandatory because they promote equality, reduce distraction, and foster school spirit.&#x27;</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Paragraph vs. Essay: A paragraph develops a single main idea with topic sentence, supporting sentences, and conclusion. An essay develops multiple related ideas across multiple paragraphs with an introduction, body, and conclusion. A paragraph is a building block of an essay, not a complete composition on its own.</td></tr><tr><td>Topic sentence vs. Thesis statement: A topic sentence introduces the main idea of a single paragraph. A thesis statement introduces the main argument of an entire essay. Both serve as central statements, but at different levels of organisation. A paragraph can have only one topic sentence; an essay has one thesis statement.</td></tr><tr><td>Descriptive vs. Narrative paragraph: A descriptive paragraph focuses on painting a sensory picture (what something looks, sounds, smells like). A narrative paragraph focuses on telling a story or recounting events (what happened, in what order). A descriptive paragraph answers &#x27;what is it like?&#x27;; a narrative paragraph answers &#x27;what happened?&#x27;.</td></tr><tr><td>Unity vs. Coherence: Unity means all sentences relate to one main idea. Coherence means the sentences flow logically and connect smoothly. A paragraph can have unity (all sentences about exercise) but lack coherence (sentences in random order). Both are required for a good paragraph.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Report Writing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is a Report?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A report is a factual account of an event, situation, or investigation, written for a specific audience (usually a teacher, principal, or authority). In the NEB Class 11 English examination, report writing typically carries 5-8 marks. Unlike essays or letters, reports are objective, formal, and structured — they present facts and observations without personal opinion or emotional language. A good report answers the questions: What happened? When? Where? Who was involved? What were the results?</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Format of a Report</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The standard report format includes: (1) Heading/Title — a clear, descriptive title (e.g., &#x27;Report on the Annual Science Exhibition&#x27;). (2) Date — the date of writing (placed below the title or at the end). (3) Introduction — briefly state the purpose of the report and the event/situation being reported. (4) Body — present the facts in a logical order (chronological, thematic, or by importance). Use headings and subheadings if the report is long. (5) Conclusion — summarise the key findings or outcomes. (6) Signature and designation — the reporter&#x27;s name and role (e.g., &#x27;Ramesh Sharma, Class Representative&#x27;). The body should be 100-150 words for NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Key Features of Report Writing</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A report must be: (1) Objective — present facts without personal opinion or emotion. Use &#x27;It was observed that...&#x27; instead of &#x27;I think that...&#x27;. (2) Concise — include only relevant information; avoid unnecessary details. (3) Organised — use headings, subheadings, and logical order (chronological or thematic). (4) Formal — use formal language; no contractions, slang, or informal expressions. (5) Third-person — write in third person, not first person. (6) Past tense — reports describe events that have already happened, so past tense is standard. These features distinguish reports from essays and narratives.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Types of Reports</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Common types of reports in NEB exams include: (1) Event report — reporting on a school event (sports day, function, visit). &#x27;The Annual Sports Day was held on 15th November. The event began with the flag hoisting ceremony...&#x27; (2) Incident report — reporting on an accident or unexpected event. &#x27;An incident occurred on 10th November when a tree fell on the school boundary wall during the night storm...&#x27; (3) Investigation report — reporting findings from an inquiry. &#x27;An inquiry was conducted into the matter of missing laboratory equipment...&#x27; (4) Progress report — reporting on the status of a project. &#x27;The construction of the new science lab is 70% complete...&#x27; Each type follows the same basic format but differs in content and focus.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Writing an Event Report</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>An event report describes a function, ceremony, or activity that took place. Structure: (1) Opening — state the event, date, venue, and purpose. &#x27;The Annual Cultural Festival of our school was held on 20th November 2024 in the school auditorium.&#x27; (2) Body — describe the key activities in chronological order. &#x27;The function began with the lighting of the ceremonial lamp by the principal. This was followed by cultural programmes including dance, drama, and song performances by students from different classes...&#x27; (3) Conclusion — summarise the outcome and express satisfaction. &#x27;The event concluded with the prize distribution ceremony at 5:00 PM. The festival was a great success, reflecting the artistic talent of the students.&#x27; Keep the language formal and factual.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Writing an Incident Report</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>An incident report records an unexpected or unfortunate event. Structure: (1) Opening — state what happened, when, and where. &#x27;An accident occurred on the morning of 5th November 2024 at approximately 8:30 AM on the school campus.&#x27; (2) Body — describe the sequence of events factually. &#x27;A student from Class 10, Ram Maharjan, slipped on a wet floor near the science laboratory and fractured his left wrist. The incident was reported immediately to the school nurse...&#x27; (3) Conclusion — state the outcome and any recommendations. &#x27;The student was admitted to nearby hospital. It is recommended that warning signs be placed on wet floors to prevent similar incidents.&#x27; Maintain an objective, impersonal tone throughout.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Report vs. Essay: A report presents facts objectively with headings and a structured format. An essay presents the author&#x27;s argument or analysis with an introduction, body paragraphs, and conclusion. Reports use past tense and third person; essays may use first person (in narrative/descriptive essays) and present tense. Reports are formal and factual; essays can be personal and argumentative.</td></tr><tr><td>Report vs. Notice: A notice is a brief announcement (50-80 words) posted publicly to inform about an event or rule. A report is a detailed account (100-150 words) of an event that has already occurred. Notices use future or present tense (announcing what will happen); reports use past tense (describing what happened). Notices have a &#x27;NOTICE&#x27; heading; reports have a descriptive title.</td></tr><tr><td>Report vs. Narrative: A report presents facts in an objective, formal tone with headings. A narrative tells a story with a plot, characters, and a chronological sequence, often using first person and emotional language. Reports answer &#x27;what happened?&#x27;; narratives answer &#x27;what happened and how did it feel?&#x27; In NEB exams, reports are always objective; narratives are personal and descriptive.</td></tr><tr><td>Third person in reports: Reports must be written in the third person (he, she, it, they, the student, the principal). Never use first person (I, we, my, our) in a report. This is because reports are objective accounts, not personal stories. Using &#x27;I&#x27; or &#x27;we&#x27; makes the report subjective and informal, which loses marks.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Email Writing</td><td>Email Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Essay Writing</td><td>Essay Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Letter Writing</td><td>Letter Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Notice Writing</td><td>Notice Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Paragraph Writing</td><td>Paragraph Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Report Writing</td><td>Report Writing covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Writing.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Strong writing and reading use evidence purposefully: a claim should be traceable to textual or factual support, and an explanation should show how that evidence answers the task. Audience and purpose shape structure and register.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>For reading, distinguish what a passage states from inference; for writing, follow the requested genre, audience and word limits. Do not infer an author’s intention or treat a single interpretation as certain without supporting evidence.</p><h3>Open research question — How evidence supports more than one reading</h3><p>Literary interpretation is evidence-based rather than a hunt for one hidden answer. A defensible open reading offers at least two text-supported interpretations, cites exact details, and explains what complicates each; authorial intention should not be asserted without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>In “Writing”, which two interpretations can the exact wording support, and what detail complicates each? Record the line or passage; leave the issue open if the text does not decide it.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://writing.wisc.edu/handbook/academicwriting/closereading/">UW–Madison: close reading</a></li><li><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/practiceguide/22">WWC: teaching secondary students to write effectively</a></li><li><a href="https://www.york.ac.uk/english/about/writing-at-york/writing-resources/close-reading/">University of York: close reading and ambiguity</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>